# New Project Workflow

## Objective

Start every new project with a consistent AI context, documentation contracts,
and ChatGPT export support.

## Checklist

1.  Select the appropriate project profile from `core/project-profiles/` and
    create/scaffold the project. Choose the stack intentionally; once
    scaffolded, treat the installed environment as the starting point.
    Current profiles: `laravel-webapp` for Laravel web applications;
    `roots-radicle` for Roots Radicle/Sage WordPress projects. Do not apply
    a profile to a project that is not that stack.
2.  Bootstrap AI Platform infrastructure into the project:
    - Preferred: from the target project root, run
      `/path/to/ai-platform/scripts/bootstrap-project.sh` with no
      argument (uses `$PWD` when invoked from outside the AI Platform
      repository).
    - Alternative: from the AI Platform repository, run
      `scripts/bootstrap-project.sh /path/to/project`.
    - Fallback: run `scripts/bootstrap-project.sh` from the AI Platform
      repository with no argument and supply the target path when
      prompted.
    Onboarding for a first-time clone is in the repository `README.md`.
3.  Establish project-specific documentation contracts **before** substantive
    feature development. Bootstrap installs templates and shared
    infrastructure only; it does not populate project truth.
    Existing projects: after bootstrap, run Project Adoption
    (`core/prompts/project-adoption.md`) in a fresh agent session.
4.  Export initial ChatGPT context and upload to your ChatGPT Project.
5.  Begin implementation.

## Bootstrap Target Resolution

`scripts/bootstrap-project.sh` resolves the target project as follows:

1. Explicit path argument, if supplied
2. Current working directory (`$PWD`) when invoked from outside the AI
   Platform repository
3. Interactive prompt as the fallback

## Bootstrap Provides

Running bootstrap on a target project:

-   Links shared Cursor rules into `.cursor/rules/`
-   Links `AGENTS.md` to `core/agent-core.md` when missing; copies
    `CLAUDE.md` when missing; links `.claude/skills/` and `.grok/skills/`
    to `.agents/skills/`; does not create `.hermes.md` (Hermes uses
    `AGENTS.md`); on Laravel apps, links `.ai/guidelines/ai-platform.md`
    to `core/agent-core.md`
-   Links `scripts/export-chatgpt-context.sh` and `scripts/doctor.sh`
-   Copies documentation templates when `docs/README.md` does not exist
-   For existing projects (docs index already present): does not overwrite
    docs; may add missing draft templates (browser QA, reconciliation)
-   Adds `.build/` to `.gitignore` when missing
-   For Laravel web apps: ensures Playwright artifact gitignore entries;
    diagnoses Laravel Boost and Playwright without installing packages
-   For new Roots/Radicle/Sage projects: ensures `resources/images/`
    exists; diagnoses the preferred greenfield stack (ACF Pro, Keen
    Slider, WPForms Lite) without installing npm packages or WordPress
    plugins
-   Restores required project skills

Bootstrap is safe to re-run on an already-bootstrapped project.
It does not run Composer, `boost:install`, add Playwright, or install
WordPress plugins or npm packages.
It does not analyze the repository or adopt it into the platform.
That work belongs to Project Adoption.

Shared Cursor rules in the target project are **symlinks** into this
repository. Project `docs/` and `.cursor/rules/90-*.mdc` are owned by
the project.

## New vs existing projects

**New** (no `docs/README.md`): copy documentation templates, including
`99-project-status.md` and a draft browser-QA contract for Laravel web
apps. Then establish contracts from the installed scaffold.

**Existing** (docs index present): do not clone the platform into the
app. Bootstrap only provisions/links the platform and may add missing
drafts (status, reconciliation, Laravel browser QA). Then run Project
Adoption in a fresh agent session:
`core/prompts/project-adoption.md`.

Do not migrate or refactor application code during bootstrap.

## Existing Project Adoption

After bootstrap, start a fresh agent session **in the target project**
and follow `core/prompts/project-adoption.md`.

Sequence: Understand → Document → Compare → Question → Recommend →
Approve → Align.

The agent may inspect the repository and create or update documentation
that describes verified existing behavior. It must **not** make material
implementation or architectural changes merely because the project
differs from AI Platform standards.

Classify findings:

- **A.** Sound — document and preserve
- **B.** Reasonable difference from platform preference — document; do
  not change merely for conformity
- **C.** Outdated, unsafe, structurally problematic, or contrary to
  current best practices — recommend a change; do not implement yet
- **D.** Cannot safely be inferred — ask the developer (grouped)

For each material **C** item, present current state, problem,
recommended state, reason, expected impact, and migration/risk, then
wait for approval. Approved work is implemented; deferred work goes on
the roadmap; rejected work is recorded as **B**.

Ask only when the answer is a genuine product, business, architectural,
security, or implementation decision. Do not ask what the repository
already establishes.

During adoption, load the full existing `docs/` set. That is an
exception to selective loading in `10-documentation`. Presenting
classified findings and grouped questions is required; it is not a
violation of the single-recommendation rule. Each **C** item still has
one recommended state.

Adoption is complete only when purpose, architecture, significant
decisions, applicable contracts, roadmap/status, and needed
`90-*.mdc` rules are in place; important deviations have been reviewed;
approved remediation is done or explicitly on the roadmap; and
documentation matches the resulting implementation.

## Post-Bootstrap: New Project Context

After bootstrap of a **new** project, an agent (or human) must inspect
the **actual** scaffold and establish project-specific documentation
contracts:

1. Inspect the project structure, installed environment, dependencies,
   architecture, MCP, tests, and any available documentation.
2. Fill `docs/00-project-context.md` and foundational contracts so they
   describe this project — not generic platform assumptions or leftover
   template placeholders.
3. Follow Current-First Engineering in `00-platform`:
   - Newly scaffolded projects: respect the selected/installed stack.
   - Do not upgrade dependencies merely because newer versions exist;
     upgrades require explicit direction or approval under platform Safety
     rules.
4. For Laravel web apps: treat missing Boost as a capability gap (native
   install, not a platform MCP patch). Treat Playwright Mode A as expected;
   do not add `@playwright/test` without approval.
5. For Roots/Radicle/Sage: follow `core/project-profiles/roots-radicle.md`.
   After Current-First compatibility checks, complete the approved
   greenfield baseline: ensure `resources/images/` exists; install
   `keen-slider` via the project's npm/Vite workflow; once WordPress is
   available, install/activate WPForms Lite via the current WP-CLI /
   WordPress mechanism; install/activate ACF Pro when the project's
   approved credential mechanism allows it, otherwise report the
   credential blocker and continue. Do not commit secrets. Do not
   silently substitute free ACF. Do not invent sliders, an ACF page
   builder, or custom form infrastructure the project does not need.
   Establish the page/menu provisioning architecture. Do not invent
   project pages until a project contract defines them. Once that
   architecture is approved, use the project provisioner rather than
   wp-admin for reproducible structure. If the initial contract already
   defines the sitemap and the application environment is operational,
   this workflow may invoke the approved provisioner. Platform
   `bootstrap-project.sh` does not provision WordPress content and does
   not install npm packages or WordPress plugins.

Do not begin substantive feature work while project contracts still contain
only unfilled template placeholders.

Existing projects use **Existing Project Adoption** above, not this
greenfield fill-in path.

## Browser QA loop (opt-in)

Laravel web applications are browser-capable and may have Playwright
Mode A plus Mode B exploration. Do **not** run that loop after feature
work unless the current task explicitly authorizes browser QA
(`60-browser-qa`).

When authorized, see `core/prompts/browser-qa-loop.md` and
`docs/contracts/features/browser-qa.md`.

## Initial Documentation Contracts

Every project must include:

-   `docs/README.md` — dependency graph and contract registry
-   `docs/00-project-context.md` — concise ChatGPT and agent entry point
-   `docs/99-project-status.md` — current implementation status (update when
    phases or major milestones complete)

Minimum viable contracts:

-   `docs/contracts/foundational/vision.md`
-   `docs/contracts/foundational/architecture.md`

Existing projects also document classified differences in
`docs/contracts/foundational/platform-reconciliation.md` during Project
Adoption. Create `.cursor/rules/90-*.mdc` when project-specific agent
guidance is warranted.

Laravel web applications also get a draft
`docs/contracts/features/browser-qa.md`. Other project types do not.

Roots/Radicle/Sage projects record page/menu architecture in a project
contract when they have one. The platform does not ship a sitemap
template. Do not invent pages during bootstrap. New Roots contracts
should also record: ACF owns structured editorial fields and is not a
page builder; frontend layout remains code-owned; Keen is the preferred
slider/gallery dependency; WPForms Lite is the default basic forms
layer; upgrades/alternatives require actual project need; credentials
are never committed.

Add engine and feature contracts as the project grows. Do not create the
legacy four-file layout for new projects.

## ChatGPT Export

After customizing documentation, from the **project** root (the script is
a bootstrap symlink into the AI Platform):

```bash
scripts/export-chatgpt-context.sh
```

If the symlink is not present, run the clone copy from that same project
root: `/path/to/ai-platform/scripts/export-chatgpt-context.sh`.

Upload `.build/chatgpt-context.zip` to the ChatGPT Project. Re-export when
documentation, Cursor rules, agent skills, or AI metadata change.

Re-check a bootstrapped project with
`/path/to/ai-platform/scripts/doctor.sh /path/to/project`. After
bootstrap, `scripts/doctor.sh` in the project is the same script via
symlink.

## Maintenance

All contracts must be maintained in accordance with the `10-documentation`
project rule.
